@page "/error"
@using System.Diagnostics
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

@*
    Anonymous, and it has to be.

    The portal denies by default, and outside Development UseExceptionHandler re-executes
    failed requests at this path. Without the attribute above, an anonymous visitor who hits
    any error is redirected here, fails the fallback policy here, and is redirected to
    /sign-in — which, if the error is not specific to one page, fails the same way again.
    That is not a broken error page, it is the whole dashboard unreachable behind a redirect
    loop that reads as ordinary sign-in traffic in the logs.

    It also cannot happen in Development, because the exception handler is not registered
    there. So it is invisible until it is in production. PortalErrorPageTests is the guard.
*@

<PageTitle>Something went wrong · Zazi</PageTitle>

<div class="signin">
    <h1>Zazi</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Something went wrong</p>

    <p>
        The page could not be loaded. Nothing you were doing has been lost — Zazi records
        transactions when they are submitted, not when a page renders.
    </p>

    <p>
        Try again in a moment. If it keeps happening, quote the reference below when you
        report it.
    </p>

    @if (ShowRequestId)
    {
        <p>
            <strong>Reference:</strong> <code>@RequestId</code>
        </p>
    }

    @* Deliberately no exception detail, no stack trace, and no advice to enable Development
       mode — which is what the stock template puts here. Telling the operator of a financial
       system to switch on the setting that prints exception detail to end users is not advice
       worth shipping. The detail is in the server log, where it belongs. *@

    <p><a href="/sign-in">Back to sign in</a></p>
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private string? RequestId { get; set; }
    private bool ShowRequestId => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(RequestId);

    // The same identifier that appears in the server log for this request, so a report of
    // "it broke" can be matched to the actual exception without asking the person anything
    // they would have to understand.
    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        RequestId = Activity.Current?.Id ?? HttpContext?.TraceIdentifier;
}
